    "https://sju.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Philadelphia---Hawk-Hill/Adjunct--English_JR101211": {
      "title": "Adjunct, English",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPosition Title: Adjunct, English Time Type: Part time Position Summary and Qualifications: Saint Joseph's University's Department of English and Writing has an ongoing need for highly qualified adjunct faculty to staff a variety of courses each semester at SJU's Hawk Hill campus. Course assignments will likely focus on first-year writing courses but may include upper-level courses in specialized courses like professional and organizational writing, public speaking, creative writing, literature, etc. Please submit a cover letter, cv/resume and statement of teaching philosophy with your application. Saint Joseph’s University (SJU) offers a wide variety of bachelor's, master's, doctoral, associate and certificate programs. With a commitment to personalized attention through a low faculty to student ratio, SJU may call on the expertise of adjunct instructors to complement the work of full-time faculty across all of its colleges and schools. SJU appoints part-time, non-tenure track adjunct instructors on a semester-to-semester basis with no guarantee of consecutive appointments. Re-appointment is based on university need, student enrollment, and satisfactory performance. Applicants for these positions are screened on an as-needed basis throughout the academic year. Teaching responsibilities are assigned by the department chair. Compensation for adjunct instructors is commensurate with educational credentials, professional, experience and instructional background. Adjunct instructors are bound to the terms and conditions of employment at Saint Joseph’s University, including but not limited to the Faculty Handbook. More information about SJU’s academic programs is available online at: www.sju.edu/degree-programs. ​ Required Materials: To complete your application, please submit a Cover Letter/Letter of Interest, a Resume/Curriculum Vitae, and a Statement of Teaching Philosophy. Essential Duties & Responsibilities: Provide support and guidance to students Instruct an assigned course providing syllabi and other instructional materials Use of university learning management systems to deliver instruction Teaching a specified course according to the Department of English and Writing guidelines. Providing support and guidance to students. Using the university learning management systems to deliver instruction. Holding regular office hours. Part-time adjunct faculty may instruct one or two courses each semester on an as-needed basis. The primary introductory course assignment will be ENG 101, The Craft of Language. Reporting mid-semester and final grades electronically. Participating in departmental course assessment as needed. Introductory courses are typically offered during the day, although evening or online courses may be available. Courses may be held on the Hawk Hill or University City campuses depending on departmental need. Knowledge of writing pedagogy is essential to teach these courses. The ability to teach in multiple modalities (face-to-face, online remote synchronous, online asynchronous) would be helpful but not a requirement. Secondary Responsibilities include: Occasional meetings with the Department Chair and other faculty members to discuss pedagogical issues. Cooperation with course coordinators or other program faculty to assure quality and consistency across the program. Minimum Qualifications: A Master's Degree in English or related field. Access to up-to-date technology. Preferred Qualifications: ABD or PhD in Literature, Rhetoric and Composition, or Creative Writing. Previous teaching experience. Willingness to be flexible in class scheduling. Facility with hybrid and online modes of instruction. Physical and Other Requirements May require standing for long periods of time May require talking for long periods of time May require carrying course materials  Modality:  Fully online OR In-person only Pay Transparency: Adjunct compensation is determined by education and relevant work experience. Learn more about the adjunct pay structure and the University's approach to pay and benefits transparency. Notice to Out of State Applicants:   Saint Joseph’s University may only consider applicants from Pennsylvania, New Jersey, New York (not including New York City zip codes), and Delaware (Delaware residents must regularly be present for over 50% of work time on an SJU campus).  If an employee must relocate to meet these conditions, financial assistance may be available but is not guaranteed. Saint Joseph’s University is a private, Catholic, Jesuit institution and we expect members of our community to be knowledgeable about – and to make a positive contribution to – our mission. Saint Joseph’s University is an equal opportunity employer that seeks to recruit, develop and retain a talented and diverse workforce. The University is committed to the diversity of its faculty and staff so that our students, our disciplines and our community as a whole can benefit from the multiple perspectives it offers. The University seeks qualified candidates who share our commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion. EOE Saint Joseph’s University prohibits discrimination on the basis of sex in its programs and activities, including admission and employment, in accordance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972. The Title IX Coordinator is responsible for overseeing compliance with Title IX and other civil rights laws and regulations. To contact the Title IX Coordinator, e-mail titleix@sju.edu, visit Campion Student Center suite 243, or call 610-660-1145. To learn more about the University’s Title IX policies, the process for filing a report or formal complaint of sex discrimination, sexual harassment, or other form of sexual misconduct, and the University’s response to reports and/or formal complaints, please visit www.sju.edu/titleix . Inquiries may also be directed to the Federal agency responsible for enforcing Title IX, the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights . Applicant Information & Disclosures Please review this article of important information pertaining (but not limited) to: Background checks, the Form I-9, Equal Opportunity Employment (EOE), and Title IX. Pay Transparency & Benefits Overview Read more about the university's approach to pay and benefits transparency ."
    },
    "https://sju.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Philadelphia---Hawk-Hill/Program-Specialist--Center-for-Global-Engagement_JR101288": {
      "title": "Program Specialist, Center for Global Engagement",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Program Specialist, Center for Global Engagement Time Type: Full time Position Summary and Qualifications: The Program Specialist for the Center for Global Engagement (CGE) assists and supports the day-to-day operations of the CGE and the Office of International Student Services (ISS) across the University campuses. The Program Specialist is responsible for the organization, management, planning, marketing, and implementation of the CEG's portfolio; and will support the advising of international students on immigration and non-academic issues, processing compliance paperwork, and developing cultural and student engagement programming for ISS. Essential Duties and Responsibilities: Supports advising of students on CGE options, as well as international students and exchange visitors on matters pertaining to their immigration status and any personal, social, cultural and/or academic concerns. Assists with and coordinates the development of programs, orientations, information sessions, events, workshops, pre-departure preparation, health and safety training, and exit interviews for both CGE and ISS. Develops promotional materials, social media content, and web-based information for international students, exchange visitors, and the CGE portfolio. Reviews and supports CGE application processing: provides follow up with incomplete applications, deposits, and pre-departure forms. Develops and prepares program fee budgets and reconciles travel expense reports for the CGE portfolio. Supports the management of records, databases, and guidelines for both CGE and ISS. Serve as a Designated School Official (DSO) for student (F-1) visa holders and as an Alternate Responsible Officer (ARO) for exchange visitor (J-1) programs. Assists with: issuing I-20 and DS-2019 forms to newly accepted students; work permission applications (CPT/OPT), changes of status, reinstatement, and other non-academic support issues; and monitoring alerts in the Student and Exchange Visitor Information System (SEVIS). Works with CGE faculty leaders to implement travel components of their courses; and with Department Chairs and Associate Deans to maintain Course Equivalency sheets. Provides continuing support for both students and faculty during travel abroad. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities: The person who holds this position has been classified as a Campus Security Authority under the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act). Coordinate delivery of services with various university offices and departments across the University. Attend and contribute to OIEIE Team meetings and planning workshops. Represent the Center for Global Engagement and the Office of International Students at some evening/weekend events. Serve on university-wide and student life committees as needed. Perform other duties as assigned by supervisor(s). Minimum Qualifications (Education/Training and Experience): Required Basic knowledge of current professional practices and issues in international education.   Excellent problem-solving skills, and ability to work independently as well as closely with team members to achieve goals. Effective verbal, written, interpersonal, and public speaking communication skills across settings and stakeholders, including diverse constituents both internally and externally. Strong organizational skills and ability to effectively evaluate details. Ability to multitask and manage multiple projects and deadlines. Demonstrated cross-cultural awareness, intercultural skills, and sensitivity to cultural concerns. Proficient with Google Suite and web-based software (e.g., Drupal). Bachelor’s degree; or equivalent professional experience. Per mandated federal regulations, must be a U.S. citizen or legal Permanent Resident. Preferred General understanding of laws relating to equal opportunity, affirmative action, discrimination, and harassment relating to faculty, staff, and students. Experience with SEVIS and basic knowledge of immigration regulations governing F, J and other visa statuses. Demonstrated commitment to institutional effectiveness, inclusive excellence, and supporting the University Mission. Experience working, living and/or studying abroad strongly preferred. Skills in planning, organizing, and managing funds, including fiscally responsible spending, analyzing trends, and ensuring compliance. Knowledge of Banner and Cognos preferred; and previous experience using Terra Dotta software is beneficial. Physical Requirements &/or Unusual Work Hours: Ability and willingness to work occasional weekend and evening hours. Ability to move about campus.. Pay Transparancy: The estimated pay range for this position is $50,000-$53,000. Offered salary will be commensurate with work experience and educational background. Click  here  to learn more about the University's approach to pay and benefits transparency. Notice to Out of State Applicants:   Saint Joseph’s University may only consider applicants from Pennsylvania, New Jersey, New York (not including New York City zip codes), and Delaware (Delaware residents must regularly be present for over 50% of work time on an SJU campus).  If an employee must relocate to meet these conditions, financial assistance may be available but is not guaranteed. Saint Joseph’s University is a private, Catholic, Jesuit institution and we expect members of our community to be knowledgeable about – and to make a positive contribution to – our mission. Saint Joseph’s University is an equal opportunity employer that seeks to recruit, develop and retain a talented and diverse workforce. The University is committed to the diversity of its faculty and staff so that our students, our disciplines and our community as a whole can benefit from the multiple perspectives it offers. The University seeks qualified candidates who share our commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion. EOE Saint Joseph’s University prohibits discrimination on the basis of sex in its programs and activities, including admission and employment, in accordance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972. The Title IX Coordinator is responsible for overseeing compliance with Title IX and other civil rights laws and regulations. To contact the Title IX Coordinator, e-mail titleix@sju.edu, visit Campion Student Center suite 243, or call 610-660-1145. To learn more about the University’s Title IX policies, the process for filing a report or formal complaint of sex discrimination, sexual harassment, or other form of sexual misconduct, and the University’s response to reports and/or formal complaints, please visit www.sju.edu/titleix . Inquiries may also be directed to the Federal agency responsible for enforcing Title IX, the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights . Applicant Information & Disclosures Please review this article of important information pertaining (but not limited) to: Background checks, the Form I-9, Equal Opportunity Employment (EOE), and Title IX. Pay Transparency & Benefits Overview Read more about the university's approach to pay and benefits transparency ."
    },
    "https://sju.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Philadelphia---Hawk-Hill/Program-Specialist--Inclusive-Excellence_JR101285": {
      "title": "Program Specialist, Inclusive Excellence",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-31\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Program Specialist, Inclusive Excellence Time Type: Full time Position Summary and Qualifications: The Program Specialist supports the mission of the Office of Inclusive Excellence by overseeing and managing programming for the Center for Inclusion and Diversity (CID) and the Cura Collective (CC) across University locations, as well as maintaining the Office’s efforts to build a sense of belonging and maintain an inclusive campus environment. This position collaborates with students, faculty, staff, and alumni to create programs, events, activities, and learning opportunities in a team-based environment toward the goal of facilitating belonging, development, learning, and engagement. Essential Duties and Responsibilities: Develop a comprehensive programming calendar that encourages community involvement and outreach for the University community. Provide programming and advising support to the student organizations for affinity populations that are funded by the Student Budget Allocated Committee (SBAC). Lead, plan, facilitate, and oversee student-centered events including the annual Center for Inclusion and Diversity Retreat, movie nights, heritage month activities, Taste of the World, Kwanzaa Celebration, Senior Celebrations, and others that may arise. Create and maintain marketing, outreach, and social media presence for the Center for Inclusion and Diversity; support and update the OIE website as needed. Process invoices, monitor budgets, and assist in tracking and reporting expenses. Collaborate with partners across University locations and represent the Office of Inclusive Excellence at Admissions Events, programming, training, Department presentations, panels, and other events as needed. Assist with outreach, professional mentoring, advocacy support, and resource referrals for students with underrepresented and under-resourced identities to increase persistence to graduation. Assist with events and activities to support diverse student learning, retention, and belonging, including the Running Start Program, Cura Collective programs, and our contributions to New Student Orientation. Serve as an active member of standing committees and ad hoc committees that address inclusive excellence, including Day of Dialogue. Support the hiring, training, and supervision of the CID and CC federal work study employees. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities: The person who holds this position has been classified as a Campus Security Authority under the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act). Attend and contribute to OIEIE Team meetings and planning workshops. Liaise with the Tri-Alpha First Generation Honor Society advisor(s). Support work in other areas of the OIEIE. Partner and work closely with various university offices and departments. Support the assessment and evaluation of student participation and professional development. Represent the Office of Inclusive Excellence at some evening/weekend events. Serve on university-wide and student life committees as needed. Other duties as assigned by supervisor(s). Minimum Qualifications (Education/Training and Experience): Required Basic knowledge and conversant understanding of inclusive excellence and student development trends, topics, and initiatives in educational settings. Demonstrated ability to work effectively with a diverse student body. Excellent problem-solving skills, and ability to work independently as well as closely with team members to achieve goals. Effective verbal, written, interpersonal, and public speaking communication skills across settings and stakeholders, including diverse constituents both internally and externally. Strong organizational skills and ability to effectively evaluate details. Ability to multitask and manage multiple projects and deadlines. Proficient with Google Suite and web-based software (e.g., Drupal). Bachelor’s degree; or equivalent and relevant professional experience. Preferred General understanding of laws relating to equal opportunity, affirmative action, discrimination, and harassment relating to faculty, staff, and students. Demonstrated commitment to institutional effectiveness, inclusive excellence, and supporting the University Mission. Skills in planning, organizing, and managing funds, including fiscally responsible spending, and analyzing trends. Knowledge of Banner and Cognos preferred. Physical Requirements &/or Unusual Work Hours: Ability and willingness to work occasional weekend and evening hours. Ability to move about campus. Pay Transparancy: The estimated pay range for this position is $50,000-$53,000. O ffered salary will be commensurate with work experience and educational background. Click  here  to learn more about the University's approach to pay and benefits transparency. Notice to Out of State Applicants:   Saint Joseph’s University may only consider applicants from Pennsylvania, New Jersey, New York (not including New York City zip codes), and Delaware (Delaware residents must regularly be present for over 50% of work time on an SJU campus).  If an employee must relocate to meet these conditions, financial assistance may be available but is not guaranteed. Saint Joseph’s University is a private, Catholic, Jesuit institution and we expect members of our community to be knowledgeable about – and to make a positive contribution to – our mission. Saint Joseph’s University is an equal opportunity employer that seeks to recruit, develop and retain a talented and diverse workforce. The University is committed to the diversity of its faculty and staff so that our students, our disciplines and our community as a whole can benefit from the multiple perspectives it offers. The University seeks qualified candidates who share our commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion. EOE Saint Joseph’s University prohibits discrimination on the basis of sex in its programs and activities, including admission and employment, in accordance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972. The Title IX Coordinator is responsible for overseeing compliance with Title IX and other civil rights laws and regulations. To contact the Title IX Coordinator, e-mail titleix@sju.edu, visit Campion Student Center suite 243, or call 610-660-1145. To learn more about the University’s Title IX policies, the process for filing a report or formal complaint of sex discrimination, sexual harassment, or other form of sexual misconduct, and the University’s response to reports and/or formal complaints, please visit www.sju.edu/titleix . Inquiries may also be directed to the Federal agency responsible for enforcing Title IX, the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights . Applicant Information & Disclosures Please review this article of important information pertaining (but not limited) to: Background checks, the Form I-9, Equal Opportunity Employment (EOE), and Title IX. Pay Transparency & Benefits Overview Read more about the university's approach to pay and benefits transparency ."
